WEBVTT

00:00:00.626 --> 00:00:05.899
The system we use today started with Carl Linnaeus, a Swedish botanist.

00:00:05.899 --> 00:00:13.696
In 1735 he published a book called Systema Naturae, which tried to organize every known plant and animal.

00:00:14.396 --> 00:00:20.300
Linnaeus sorted organisms by their morphology, meaning their physical form and structure.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.816
Organisms that looked alike went into the same group.

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:30.491
But looks can be misleading, so biologists have to ask why two organisms share a trait.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.306
Homologous traits are similar because they were inherited from a common ancestor.

00:00:36.306 --> 00:00:43.177
A human arm, a bat wing, and a whale flipper do different jobs, but they're built from the same set of bones.

00:00:43.177 --> 00:00:46.015
That shared plan points to a shared ancestor.

00:00:46.715 --> 00:00:52.487
Analogous traits are similar because they do the same job, not because of shared ancestry.

00:00:52.487 --> 00:01:00.058
Bird wings and insect wings both allow flight, but they evolved separately and are built in completely different ways.

00:01:00.058 --> 00:01:04.805
Analogous traits can trick you into grouping unrelated organisms together.

00:01:05.515 --> 00:01:13.865
Today, taxonomists combine physical traits with DNA evidence to figure out which similarities really come from a common ancestor.
